WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.124
Lesson one point one.

2
00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.758
How a guitar note becomes a drum hit, from

3
00:00:03.758 --> 00:00:05.100
the string to the speaker.

4
00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:11.222
A drummer playing with a guitarist has to

5
00:00:11.222 --> 00:00:13.554
answer almost at once: within about one

6
00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:14.331
sixteenth note.

7
00:00:14.642 --> 00:00:16.921
At a hundred and twenty beats per minute,

8
00:00:16.921 --> 00:00:18.682
that is a hundred and twenty-five

9
00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:19.356
milliseconds.

10
00:00:19.904 --> 00:00:21.880
The hard part is hearing the guitar.

11
00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.570
One microphone hears all six strings mixed

12
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.064
together.

13
00:00:25.394 --> 00:00:27.754
The low E string's third harmonic sits two

14
00:00:27.754 --> 00:00:29.895
cents away from the B string, so one

15
00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:32.146
microphone sees one peak where there are

16
00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:32.695
two notes.

17
00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:34.581
Look closely at this peak.

18
00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:37.340
Twelve hundred times the log, base two, of

19
00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.578
the ratio of the two frequencies: two

20
00:00:39.578 --> 00:00:39.890
cents.

21
00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:42.389
In the mixed signal the two notes fall in

22
00:00:42.389 --> 00:00:43.118
the same peak.

23
00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.512
In the GuitarSet recordings of solos,

24
00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:47.751
about a third of all notes overlap another

25
00:00:47.751 --> 00:00:48.115
string.

26
00:00:48.999 --> 00:00:50.317
So we didn't solve that problem.

27
00:00:50.565 --> 00:00:51.141
We removed it.

28
00:00:57.147 --> 00:01:00.034
A hexaphonic pickup, the Roland GK-3, puts

29
00:01:00.034 --> 00:01:02.308
one small magnetic pickup under each

30
00:01:02.308 --> 00:01:02.738
string.

31
00:01:03.106 --> 00:01:05.318
Six strings, six separate signals.

32
00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:08.294
Sena's circuit board, the PCB, amplifies

33
00:01:08.294 --> 00:01:10.746
each one, filters it, and turns it into

34
00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:11.192
numbers.

35
00:01:15.627 --> 00:01:17.956
The FPGA watches all six strings at once.

36
00:01:18.297 --> 00:01:20.684
For each one, it measures the energy in

37
00:01:20.684 --> 00:01:21.423
short frames.

38
00:01:21.764 --> 00:01:24.150
When the energy jumps past a threshold,

39
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.480
that's an onset, and a packet goes out

40
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:27.276
straight away.

41
00:01:28.015 --> 00:01:30.661
Pitch takes longer: the board has to see a

42
00:01:30.661 --> 00:01:32.976
few full cycles of the wave before it can

43
00:01:32.976 --> 00:01:33.747
name the note.

44
00:01:34.078 --> 00:01:36.448
On the low E string one cycle lasts twelve

45
00:01:36.448 --> 00:01:38.818
milliseconds, so the plan puts its pitch

46
00:01:38.818 --> 00:01:41.023
about thirty-five to fifty milliseconds

47
00:01:41.023 --> 00:01:41.905
after the onset.

48
00:01:42.437 --> 00:01:44.630
Vindy built the onset detector, Felix the

49
00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:45.378
pitch detector.

50
00:01:47.097 --> 00:01:49.152
Two packets matter to the laptop:

51
00:01:49.152 --> 00:01:50.943
thirty-two bytes for the onset,

52
00:01:50.943 --> 00:01:53.051
forty-eight for the pitch, which most

53
00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:53.577
notes get.

54
00:01:53.894 --> 00:01:56.159
The board also sends an offset packet when

55
00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:56.791
a note ends.

56
00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:03.952
They travel over Ethernet to the laptop,

57
00:02:03.952 --> 00:02:06.430
as UDP packets to port five thousand and

58
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:06.732
five.

59
00:02:07.094 --> 00:02:09.391
A ping takes well under a millisecond.

60
00:02:09.909 --> 00:02:11.982
One surprise: the link only works at

61
00:02:11.982 --> 00:02:12.672
gigabit speed.

62
00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:15.139
That's not about bandwidth, because a few

63
00:02:15.139 --> 00:02:16.916
small packets a second would fit on

64
00:02:16.916 --> 00:02:17.360
anything.

65
00:02:17.656 --> 00:02:19.876
At a hundred megabits, the likely cause,

66
00:02:19.876 --> 00:02:22.097
read from the driver's code, is a firmware

67
00:02:22.097 --> 00:02:24.120
setting that leaves the board's transmit

68
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.340
clock at the wrong rate, and nothing valid

69
00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:26.833
comes out.

70
00:02:30.657 --> 00:02:33.034
On the laptop, each onset is rounded to

71
00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:35.185
its nearest slot, exactly as in the

72
00:02:35.185 --> 00:02:35.977
training data.

73
00:02:36.499 --> 00:02:38.536
The drum model reads five numbers per

74
00:02:38.536 --> 00:02:40.680
slot, which the next lesson covers in

75
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:41.055
detail.

76
00:02:41.376 --> 00:02:43.198
Notice what's missing: pitch.

77
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.771
The drums only listen to when notes start.

78
00:02:46.092 --> 00:02:47.218
Pitch feeds the bass.

79
00:02:49.058 --> 00:02:50.106
Here's the timing.

80
00:02:50.455 --> 00:02:52.783
Slot t is decided from the guitar up to

81
00:02:52.783 --> 00:02:54.937
slot t minus one, and it's decided

82
00:02:54.937 --> 00:02:57.090
thirty-seven and a half milliseconds

83
00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.429
before it has to sound.

84
00:02:58.925 --> 00:03:00.522
The model's step takes about a

85
00:03:00.522 --> 00:03:01.140
millisecond.

86
00:03:01.449 --> 00:03:03.045
The audio system reports about

87
00:03:03.045 --> 00:03:05.157
twenty-three milliseconds to get a sound

88
00:03:05.157 --> 00:03:07.011
out of the speaker, so the hit is

89
00:03:07.011 --> 00:03:08.968
scheduled to land exactly on its grid

90
00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:09.225
line.

91
00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:11.543
If a hit would be late, it's dropped.

92
00:03:11.852 --> 00:03:13.036
It's never played late.

93
00:03:15.086 --> 00:03:17.276
Seven drummers can be switched in at the

94
00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:19.734
next bar line, and a simple rule bass, so

95
00:03:19.734 --> 00:03:21.871
far tried only with a fake board, can

96
00:03:21.871 --> 00:03:22.725
follow the kick.

97
00:03:26.627 --> 00:03:28.822
Here is the whole budget against one slot.

98
00:03:29.135 --> 00:03:31.696
Measured, and drawn solid: the network,

99
00:03:31.696 --> 00:03:32.428
and the model.

100
00:03:32.742 --> 00:03:34.884
The audio's twenty-three milliseconds,

101
00:03:34.884 --> 00:03:36.923
drawn pale, is the system's reported

102
00:03:36.923 --> 00:03:39.327
figure: only the round trip out and back

103
00:03:39.327 --> 00:03:40.163
in was measured.

104
00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:43.101
The dashed parts we haven't measured yet:

105
00:03:43.101 --> 00:03:45.097
how long the board takes to report an

106
00:03:45.097 --> 00:03:47.250
onset, and the full time from pluck to

107
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:47.828
drum sound.

108
00:03:48.347 --> 00:03:50.315
By the arithmetic, a pluck is answered

109
00:03:50.315 --> 00:03:52.331
between half a slot and a slot and a half

110
00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:52.619
later.

111
00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.216
Where does it stand?

112
00:03:58.509 --> 00:04:00.462
The laptop half has run live, in real

113
00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:02.512
time, with a fake board standing in for

114
00:04:02.512 --> 00:04:02.952
the FPGA.

115
00:04:03.245 --> 00:04:05.295
The real board has sent real notes to the

116
00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:05.637
laptop.

117
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.785
And real playing has gone through all

118
00:04:07.785 --> 00:04:09.786
seven drummers, but only as recordings

119
00:04:09.786 --> 00:04:10.372
played back.

120
00:04:10.665 --> 00:04:12.716
The last step, a guitarist playing into

121
00:04:12.716 --> 00:04:14.815
the board with the drums answering live,

122
00:04:14.815 --> 00:04:15.547
hasn't run yet.

123
00:04:17.461 --> 00:04:19.947
So: a pluck becomes an onset packet, and

124
00:04:19.947 --> 00:04:21.812
the drums answer from the next slot.

125
00:04:22.123 --> 00:04:24.558
Next: the slot grid, where the laptop's

126
00:04:24.558 --> 00:04:25.180
half begins.

